        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The majority of humans are infected with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) early in life and thereafter carry the virus in a latent form. Reactivation of latent EBV may be an important threat to crew health during extended space missions. EBV is the causative agent of infectious mononucleosis as well as nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Burkitt’s lymphoma, and other B-lymphocyte lymphomas in immunosuppressed individuals. Control of replication <i>in vivo</i> is mediated primarily by EBV specific cytotoxic T-lymphocytes, and severe clinical symptoms have been associated with reactivation of latent viruses in patients with defective cellular immunity. Decreased cellular immune function has been reported both during and after space flight. Preliminary studies have demonstrated increased EBV shedding in saliva as well as increased antibody titers to EBV lytic proteins.
<p>
The objective of this investigation was to examine the mechanisms of space flight induced alterations in human immune function and latent virus reactivation.  Specifically, this study determined the magnitude of immunosuppression as a result of space flight by: (1) analysis of stress hormones and cytokines; (2) quantitative analysis of Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) replication using molecular and serological methods; and (3) determination of virus-specific T-cell immune function.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The study protocol was approved in advance by the Committee for the Protection of Human Subjects at Johnson Space Center (JSC) and each subject provided written informed consent before participating. Eight separate samples of peripheral blood were collected from 63 astronauts; 45 flew on fourteen Space Shuttle missions and18 flew on eight ISS missions. The average length of the Shuttle missions was approximately 11 days while the average length of the ISS missions was approximately 180 days. Peripheral blood was collected 180 days before launch (L-180), L-65-44, L-10, L-2, within 4 hours after return/landing R+0, R+3, R+14-30, and R+180. Preflight blood draws were performed at the JSC, Houston, TX. Postflight blood draws for Shuttle crewmembers occurred at Kennedy Space Center (KSC), FL, or Edwards Air Force Base, CA. For ISS crewmembers, postflight blood samples were collected at KSC or Star City, Russia. Plasma was obtained after centrifugation and was stored at -70 degrees Celsius until testing. Twenty-four hour urine collections took place during the blood draws; aliquots of 24 hour pools were stored at -20 degrees Celsius until analysis.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
<b>Neuroendocrine responses after short- and long-duration space flight</b><br>
Adrenocortical responses for short-duration Shuttle and long-duration ISS astronauts vary. In Shuttle crewmembers, plasma cortisol was relatively unchanged until L-2, at which time a significant increase was observed. Plasma cortisol was not significantly increased postflight. There was a trend for increased urinary cortisol prior to flight; a significant increase in urinary cortisol was found at landing and returned to preflight levels by R+3. Urinary epinephrine was not increased at landing. However, a significant decrease in urinary epinephrine was found at R+3. 
<p>
For ISS crewmembers, plasma cortisol was not increased at any time prior to launch but was
significantly increased at landing.  Urinary cortisol was not increased preflight but it was significantly increased at landing. Both plasma and urinary cortisol returned to preflight levels within 3 days of landing. Urinary epinephrine was significantly increased at landing 
and returned to preflight levels by R+3. For urinary norepinephrine, significant elevations were found postflight (R+3 and R+15).  These elevations were resolved by R+180.
<p>
<b>Leukocyte and lymphocyte subsets</b><br>
There were significant changes in leukocyte and lymphocyte subsets both before and after short-duration shuttle flights. There were significant increases in white blood cells (WBCs) even before flight at L-2. WBCs were also increased post-landing at R+0 and R+3. Neutrophils displayed a similar pattern, increasing at L-2, nearly doubling at R+0, and remained elevated through R+3. Lymphocytes were significantly increased at L-2 but significantly decreased at R+0. Similarly, eosinophils were increased at L-2 and decreased at R+0. Monocytes were elevated preflight (L-2) and postflight (R+0) timepoints. For NK cells, there was a significant increase at L-2 but at R+0 and R+3 they were significantly decreased. B-cells were significantly increased at L-2 and R+3. Although increased at landing, this was not significant due to intrasubject variability. Total T-cells were increased at L-2 but decreased at R+0. Accordingly, both cytotoxic T-cells and helper T-cells were increased at L-2 and decreased at R+0.
<p>
For ISS crewmembers, WBCs were significantly increased at R+0 and R+3. Neutrophils were increased at R+0 and eosinophils were decreased at R+0. There were no preflight or postflight changes in lymphocytes or monocytes. For lymphocyte subsets, there were no significant changes in either NK cells or CD8+ T-cells. A significant increase in B-cells was observed at R+3. Similarly, there was a significant increase in total T-cells at R+3 which was due to a significant increase in CD4+ T-cells.
<p>
Because many prior studies have reported percent changes instead of absolute changes, the percent changes are also presented. For short-duration shuttle missions, the only significant changes in leukocyte and lymphocytes percentages occurred at landing and included: 1) increased neutrophils and B-cells; and 2) decreased eosinophils, monocytes, lymphocytes, NK cells, and CD8+ T-cells. For long-duration ISS missions, significant changes in the percentage of cell populations at landing included: 1) increased neutrophils; and 2) decreased
eosinophils, monocytes, and lymphocytes at both R+0. There were no other timepoints with significant changes. No significant changes were observed in NKs, B-cells, total T-cells, CD8+
T-cells, or CD4+ T-cells at L-2 or any time post-landing. 
<p>
<b> Plasma IL-10 levels</b><br>
For Shuttle crewmembers, IL-10 was significantly increased at L-2 as compared to L-180; no increase in IL-10 was observed postflight. For ISS crewmembers, three did not have detectable levels of IL-10 throughout the mission, and two of the crewmembers had large increases in circulating IL-10 at L-2 (56% and 96%); these same two subjects had even greater increases at R+3 (86% and 371%, respectively). No significant increase in IL-10 was found at L-2 or any individual post-flight timepoint as compared to L-180. However, grouped mean postflight IL-10 levels were significantly higher than mean preflight levels. 
<p>
<b>EBV antibody titers</b><br>
The investigators measured anti-EBV antibodies in plasma from Shuttle and ISS crewmember samples. Anti-VCA and anti-EA titers were measured in Shuttle and ISS crewmember samples.
For Shuttle crewmembers, a significant decrease was found between preflight (L-65 thru L-2) and the R+180 timepoint. This would indicate that stress-induced increases in VCA already occurred, and by the next baseline EBV reactivation had subsided. For EA, a significant increase in EA was found at L-2 through R+15 as compared to L-180 samples, indicating that lytic replication of EBV was occurring prior to and just after launch as compared to baseline samples.
For ISS, no significant changes were found in VCA antibody titers. For EA, a non-significant increase was found at L-2 as compared to L-180.
<p>
<b>EBV-specific T-cells</b><br>
The investigators measured EBV-specific CD8+ T-cells in both shuttle and ISS crewmembers. There was an increase in EBV tetramer-positive cells in shuttle crewmembers at L-2 and R+0 as compared to L-180 which resolved by R+180. Similarly, there was an increase in EBV tetramer-positive cells at L-2 in ISS crewmembers. These levels resolved by R+3.
<p>
<b> EBV T-cell function</b><br>
For Shuttle crewmembers, a significant decrease in EBV functional cells was found at L-2 and R+0. Notably, levels of cortisol were elevated at these timepoints. For ISS crewmembers, the only time the percent functional EBV tetramer+ cells significantly decreased was at R+0, at which time there were also significant elevations in plasma and urinary cortisol and urinary epinephrine.
<p>
<b> EBV DNA Levels </b><br>
There was a significant increase in viral DNA in shuttle crewmembers by L-10 which increased further by L-2. As compared to L-180, no other significant changes were found. Notably, the levels of DNA were lowest at R+180; this may signal the lowest stress level as the mission has already occurred. Similarly, there was an increase in EBV DNA at L-2 in ISS crewmembers. There was also an increase in EBV DNA at R+15. However, due to the low subject number and
intrasubject variation, none of these changes reached statistical significance. However, viral load was much greater in ISS crewmembers (when detectable) than in samples from ISS crewmembers. EBV DNA levels also increased after long-duration spaceflight, while they decreased after short-duration space flight. This suggested greater immunosuppression after long-duration flight, perhaps due to greater stress responses postflight.
<p>
<b>T-cell function</b><br>
For short-duration Space Shuttle crewmembers the mean percentage of both T cell subsets that could be stimulated to coexpress both activation markers was relatively consistent throughout all pre-flight assessments.  Following landing day (R+0) assessments, a significant increase in the percentage of T cell subsets that could be activated to express CD69 or co-express CD69/CD25 was observed, that resolved to baseline levels by R+14.  For ISS crewmembers, the mean percentage of both T cell subsets that could be stimulated to full activation (CD69+/CD25+) was also stable between both pre-flight timepoints.  However, landing day assessments for the ISS crewmembers revealed a significant decrease in percentage of both CD4+ and CD8+ T cells that could be stimulated to co-express CD69/CD25.  The total percentage of T cell subsets expressing CD69 alone was also reduced, but not significantly different, on landing day. Thus, expression of the early activation marker CD69 was relatively unchanged, but the expression of CD25 (requiring new mRNA expression) was significantly decreased. Values for the ISS crewmembers for this assay trended to resolution at R+3 and had fully returned to baseline values by R+30.
<p>
<b>Intracellular cytokine analysis</b><br>
For the short-duration shuttle crewmembers, there was a significant decrease in the percentage of all four cytokine-producing T cell subsets at R+0 as compared to the pre-mission baseline values. All the observed R+0 intracellular cytokine alterations appeared to fully resolve by the R+14 timepoint. For ISS crewmembers, there a significant decrease in the percentage of CD4+/IL-2+ T cells at R+0 as compared to the pre-mission baseline value. In contrast to the shuttle crewmembers, there was no significant decrease in the percentage of CD8+/IL-2+, CD4+/IFNg+ or CD8+/IFNg+ T cells. There did appear to be some trends towards reduced levels of CD8+ T cells making both cytokines. 
<p>
<b> Secreted Th1/Th2 cytokine analysis</b><br>
Cytokine expression data are generated as mean fluorescence intensity, and since six cytokines are assayed simultaneously, relative comparisons may be made between the cytokines. Also, since all samples were frozen and assayed simultaneously per mission, relative comparisons (per crewmember) across the data points are valid. For the shuttle crewmembers, expression of IFNg usually surpassed all other cytokines at baseline testing, yet there was a statistically significant drop in IFNg expression on R+0 that trended to resolve by R+14. Interestingly, relative expression of IFNg was lower pre-flight for the ISS (as compared to shuttle) crewmembers, but also did drop on landing day whereas other cytokines appeared unchanged. Postflight recovery data for the ISS crewmembers revealed IFNg expression that approached the Shuttle crewmembers in intensity. Expression of IL-10 among the ISS crewmembers was actually elevated at R+0, and trended downwards to baseline values by R+30. 
<p>
For the shuttle crewmembers, the greatest elevation of IFNg and IL-2 expression was seen at the L-10 and R+3 data points. No other consistent pattern of altered production was observed in the shuttle crewmembers. For the ISS crewmembers, levels of IFNg and IL-2 expression were also highest at R+3. Interestingly, levels of TNFa, which were minimal at all preflight points for the ISS crewmembers, was also significantly elevated at R+3. These elevations were generally trending to resolve by R+30. Given that an IFNg:IL-10 ratio may be an indicator of Th1:Th2 balance, such a ratio was calculated using the CD3/CD28 culture data for all crewmembers. For most shuttle crewmembers, a relatively depressed ration is observed at R+0, as compared to both pre-flight baseline values and postflight recovery data. For 4 of 5 ISS crewmembers tested, a similar shift was detected, but to an even more dramatic level: R+0 IFNg:IL-10 rations were dramatically reduced compared to both preflight and post-flight recovery data. To check for inter subject and assay variability, four healthy control subjects were evaluated and relatively similar
timepoints. 
<p>
        </div>

